<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Inverse Matrix Activity</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->

  <!-- Exercises start here. -->
<exercise workspace="2in">
    <title>Find the Inverse</title>

    <p>

            <me>
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1 &amp; 1 \\
                4 &amp; 4
                \end{bmatrix}\quad
                \begin{bmatrix}
                2 &amp; 1 \\
                5 &amp; 3
                \end{bmatrix}
         \quad
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1 &amp; 3\\
                1 &amp; 3
                \end{bmatrix}
          </me><me>
                \begin{bmatrix}
                4 &amp; -2 \\
                1 &amp; 3
                \end{bmatrix}
        \quad
                \begin{bmatrix}
                1&amp; 2 \\
                2 &amp; 4
                \end{bmatrix}
        
    </me></p>
<ol>
        <li>Compute the inverses if possible.</li>
        <li>How can you tell whether a <m>2\times2</m> matrix has no inverse?</li>
</ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
    <title>Verify the Inverse</title>
    <p>
        <m>
        A=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        2 &amp; 1\\
        3 &amp; 2
        \end{bmatrix}
        \qquad
        B=
        \begin{bmatrix}
        2 &amp; -1\\
        -3 &amp; 2
        \end{bmatrix}
        </m>
    </p>
  <ol>
        <li>Compute <m>A\cdot B</m></li>
        <li>Compute <m>B\cdot A</m></li>
        <li>If two matrices are inverses, what should their product be?</li>
</ol>


</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
    <title>Error Analysis</title>

    <p>A student found the inverse below.</p>

    <p>
        Original matrix:
        <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}
        3 &amp; 1\\
        2 &amp; 5
        \end{bmatrix}
        </m>
    </p>

    <p>
        Student's work:
        <m>
        A^{-1}=
        \frac{1}{13}
        \begin{bmatrix}
        5 &amp; 1\\
        2 &amp; 3
        \end{bmatrix}
        </m>
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Identify the mistake(s).</li>
        <li>Find the correct inverse.</li>
        <li>Explain the correct procedure for finding the inverse of a <m>2\times2</m> matrix.</li>
    </ol>

</exercise>

<exercise workspace="2in">
    <title> Solve a Matrix Equation</title>

    <p>Use inverse matrices to solve for <m>X</m>.</p>

    <p>
        <m>
        \begin{bmatrix}
        2 &amp; 1\\
        1 &amp; 1
        \end{bmatrix}
        X
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        7 &amp;1\\
        4 &amp;1
        \end{bmatrix}
        </m>
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Find the inverse of the coefficient matrix.</li>
        <li>Multiply both sides by the inverse(from left or right?).</li>
        <li>State the solution matrix.</li>
    </ol>
</exercise>

<exercise>
    <title> Solve a Matrix Equation</title>

    <p>Use inverse matrices to solve for <m>X</m>.</p>

    <p>
        <me>
        \begin{bmatrix}
        3 &amp; 2\\
        1 &amp; 4
        \end{bmatrix}
             \begin{bmatrix}
        x\\
        y
        \end{bmatrix}
        =
        \begin{bmatrix}
        16\\
        18
        \end{bmatrix}
        </me>
        Where:

x = number of Product A sold
y = number of Product B sold
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li>Use an inverse matrix to solve for x and y.</li><li>
Interpret your answer in context.</li><li>
Explain why an inverse matrix method might not work.</li>
    </ol>

    </exercise>
</handout>
